  <section class="section container prose">
    <h1>About AIToolvise</h1>

    <h2>The Person Behind This Site</h2>
    <p><strong>My name is Muhammad Saad.</strong> I built AIToolvise myself, and I run every part of it myself — the SEO, the content, the reviews, and the code the site is built on. There's no editorial team, no freelance writers churning out lists they've never actually opened, no agency behind the scenes. Just me, testing tools and writing down what I actually found.</p>

    <h2>Why AIToolvise Exists</h2>
    <p>There are thousands of AI and SaaS tools launching every month, and most sites ranking for them are running the same playbook: pull the feature list off the pricing page, add some generic praise, drop an affiliate link, publish — without ever actually using the product. I built AIToolvise to do the opposite. Every review here comes from a tool I've personally signed up for, actually used, and formed a real opinion on — not copy rewritten from a press release.</p>

    <p>Doing the SEO, the writing, and the development myself means I control the whole experience end to end — the reviews aren't outsourced, and neither is the site they live on.</p>

    <h2>How I Review Tools</h2>
    <p>Every review on AIToolvise follows the same process, whether the tool is free or costs $200 a month:</p>
    <p><strong>I sign up for real.</strong> Not a demo account someone hands me, not screenshots from a press kit — I create an account the same way you would, usually starting on the free tier or trial if one exists, then upgrading to see what actually changes.</p>
    <p><strong>I check the pricing page against the checkout page.</strong> A lot of tools advertise one number and charge another once you add seats, credits, or "add-ons" that turn out to be essential. If there's a gap between what's marketed and what you actually pay, that goes in the review.</p>
    <p><strong>I use it for a real task, not a test task.</strong> Instead of typing "hello world" into an AI tool and calling it tested, I try to use it the way an actual reader would — writing real content, generating real images, running a real workflow — so the review reflects real output quality, not a five-minute impression.</p>
    <p><strong>I look for the friction points.</strong> Confusing onboarding, credit systems that don't clearly say how far your money goes, support that takes three days to respond, features that are advertised but gated behind a higher plan — these are the things marketing pages don't mention and reviews often skip.</p>
    <p><strong>I score out of 5</strong> based on a few consistent factors: how good the actual output/results are, whether the pricing is honest and easy to understand, how easy the tool is to actually use, and how it holds up against the other tools I've reviewed in the same category. A tool doesn't get a high score for having a generous affiliate program — it gets a high score for being good.</p>
    <p>If I run out of time to test something as thoroughly as I'd like, I say so in the review rather than pretending otherwise.</p>

    <h2>What You'll Find Here</h2>
    <p>AIToolvise is organized into a few clear categories, so you can find what you actually need instead of digging through everything:</p>
    <p><strong>Reviews</strong> — full, hands-on breakdowns of a single AI or SaaS tool, tested by me directly, with an honest score out of 5.<br>
    <strong>Best Tools</strong> — curated lists for a specific use case (like "best AI video tools" or "best AI writing tools") — built from tools I've actually used, not just everything with an affiliate program.<br>
    <strong>Compare</strong> — head-to-head breakdowns when two tools keep showing up in the same searches and people can't tell them apart from the marketing pages alone.<br>
    <strong>How-To</strong> — practical guides for actually using these tools well once you've picked one — setup, workflows, tips that aren't obvious from the tool's own documentation.<br>
    <strong>Blog</strong> — broader thoughts on the AI and SaaS space, trends, and things I run into while testing tools that don't fit neatly into a review.</p>

    <h2>The Tools I Use to Build This</h2>
    <p>Since this whole site is a one-person operation, I lean on other tools myself to keep up with the workload — and honestly, that's part of what qualifies me to write about this space. I use Claude for drafting and structuring content and for help with the site's code, Rankability to guide on-page SEO and keep content aligned with what's actually ranking, and Ideogram for image generation where I need custom visuals rather than stock screenshots.</p>

    <p>Full transparency: Claude and Rankability are also affiliate partners linked elsewhere on this site, meaning I may earn a commission if you sign up through those links. I used both tools daily before they were affiliate partners and would keep using them either way — but I'd rather say that outright than have you find out later and wonder.</p>

    <p>None of that replaces the actual review process — I still sign up for and use every tool I cover myself, and the opinions and scores are mine. But I'm not going to pretend a solo site covering this many tools runs on manual work alone. If anything, using these tools daily is part of why I can speak to what's actually good in this space — I'm a user of AI and SaaS tools too, not just someone writing about them from the outside.</p>

    <h2>How I Make Money</h2>
    <p>Some of the links on this site are affiliate links, meaning I may earn a commission if you sign up through them — at no extra cost to you. This never changes a rating or a recommendation. A tool doesn't get a better score because it pays more, and a tool I wouldn't personally use doesn't get recommended because it has an affiliate program. See the <a href="/affiliate-disclosure">Affiliate Disclosure</a> for the full details.</p>

    <h2>Talk to Me</h2>
    <p>Found something inaccurate? Have a tool you think deserves a review? Disagree with a score? <a href="/contact">Contact me</a> — I read everything and reply to most of it myself, since there's no support team to route it through.</p>

    <p>This site exists because bad reviews cost people time and money. I'd rather you trust the ratings here because they're earned, not because they're the first result.</p>

    <p><strong>— Muhammad Saad</strong>, Founder of AIToolvise</p>

